# CHANGELOG.md is append-at-the-top by construction: every branch adds its entry
# under the same `## [Unreleased]` heading, so any two branches that both changed
# it conflict on the same lines — even though the entries never contradict each
# other and the correct resolution is always "keep both".
#
# Measured on the runner 2026-09-04: three repair branches (kit#19, kit#23,
# kit#24) each conflicted here, and `fleet_lander.py` refused all three on every
# pass. Three consecutive passes burned ~20 minutes each running two suites per
# branch before reaching the merge that could never apply — an hour of fleet time
# spent proving branches correct that git would not let land regardless.
#
# `merge=union` keeps both sides. For a changelog that is the right answer: the
# entries are independent facts about independent changes, and losing either one
# loses a record of something that shipped. It can duplicate a line when two
# branches write the identical text, which is visible in review and is a far
# cheaper failure than a fleet stalled on a conflict nobody is awake to resolve.
CHANGELOG.md merge=union
